## Option 1: Using Fetch API (POST request)

```javascript
async function sendMessageWithFetch(message, model) {
    const responseDiv = document.getElementById('response');
    responseDiv.textContent = '';

    const response = await fetch('/chat/stream-simple', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify({ message, model }),
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
        console.error('Error:', response.statusText);
        return;
    }

    const reader = response.body.getReader();
    const decoder = new TextDecoder();

    while (true) {
        const { done, value } = await reader.read();
        if (done) break;

        const chunk = decoder.decode(value);
        const lines = chunk.split('\n');

        for (const line of lines) {
            if (line.startsWith('data: ')) {
                const data = line.slice(6);
                if (data === '[DONE]') {
                    break;
                }

                try {
                    const parsed = JSON.parse(data);
                    // Assuming the backend sends { "token": ... } or { "full_response": ... }
                    if (parsed.token) {
                        responseDiv.textContent += parsed.token;
                    } else if (parsed.full_response) {
                        responseDiv.textContent = parsed.full_response;
                    }
                } catch (e) {
                    console.error('Error parsing JSON:', e);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Option 2: Using axios with `onDownloadProgress`

We'll set `responseType: 'text'` and then process the incoming data in the `onDownloadProgress` callback. However, note that the data we get in `onDownloadProgress` is the raw text, and we have to parse the SSE format.

But note: the `onDownloadProgress` callback in axios is called with chunks of the response, but the chunks might not be aligned with the SSE messages. So we have to buffer the incoming data and split by lines.

We can use the `axios` library and do:

```javascript
import axios from 'axios';

function sendMessageWithAxios(message, model) {
    const responseDiv = document.getElementById('response');
    responseDiv.textContent = '';

    axios({
        method: 'post',
        url: '/chat/stream-simple',
        data: { message, model },
        responseType: 'text',
        onDownloadProgress: (progressEvent) => {
            // We get chunks of data in progressEvent.event.target.response
            // But note: this is the entire response so far, so we need to track what we've already processed.
            // Alternatively, we can use the `data` field in the event, but it's not available in all browsers.

            // Instead, we can use the `currentTarget` which has the response text so far.
            const responseText = progressEvent.event.currentTarget.responseText;

            // We need to parse the SSE format from the entire responseText.
            // But note: the entire responseText is the concatenation of all chunks so far.
            // We can split by double new lines to get events, but note that an event might be split across chunks.

            // A more robust way is to split by lines and then process each line.
            const lines = responseText.split('\n');
            for (const line of lines) {
                if (line.startsWith('data: ')) {
                    const data = line.slice(6);
                    if (data === '[DONE]') {
                        break;
                    }

                    try {
                        const parsed = JSON.parse(data);
                        if (parsed.token) {
                            responseDiv.textContent += parsed.token;
                        } else if (parsed.full_response) {
                            responseDiv.textContent = parsed.full_response;
                        }
                    } catch (e) {
                        // Ignore JSON parse errors for incomplete data
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }).then(() => {
        // Request completed
    }).catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}
```

However, note that the `onDownloadProgress` in axios does not give us the raw chunks but the entire response so far. This means we might be processing the same event multiple times. We need to keep track of the last processed index.

Alternatively, we can use a buffer to accumulate the data and then split by lines, but note that the SSE format uses double newlines to separate events. We can use a stateful parser.

But given the complexity, and since the Fetch API provides a cleaner way to stream, I would recommend using the Fetch API for streaming.

## Conclusion

For streaming LLM responses with SSE, using the Fetch API is more straightforward and supported in modern browsers. If you are required to use axios, you can use the `onDownloadProgress` method, but note the challenges of parsing the SSE format from the entire response text so far.

